<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as URNG</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity (URNG) was a coalition of four leftist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> organisations formed in 1982 to fight the military governments in the civil war. After years of negotiations mediated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> it signed the final peace agreement with the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-arzu">Álvaro Arzú</a> in December 1996, ending a 36-year conflict, and it then became a political party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guatemalan-national-revolutionary-unity.jpg" alt="Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MrPenguin20 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Guatemalan_National_Revolutionary_Unity.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The civil war is usually dated from a failed coup by dissident officers on 13 November 1960; it lasted 36 years and claimed an estimated 200,000 lives. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> group, the Rebel Armed Forces (FAR), was founded in 1962. In the early 1970s two splinters of the FAR, the Organization of People in Arms (ORPA) and the Guerrilla Army of the Poor (EGP), turned to indigenous communities as their base, the EGP influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberation-theology">liberation theology</a> and strong in El Quiché.</p><p>In 1978 the EGP, the FAR and a faction of the Guatemalan Party of Labour (PGT) agreed to cooperate. The Cuban and Nicaraguan governments, which supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a>, pressed for a fuller merger, and on 8 February 1982 the EGP, ORPA, the FAR and the National Directing Nucleus of the PGT formed the URNG under Cuban mediation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A month after its founding the URNG faced the scorched-earth campaign of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/efrain-rios-montt">Efraín Ríos Montt</a>, who held power until August 1983. In the Guatemalan genocide of mid 1981 to 1983 about 150,000 civilians were killed and 1.5 million displaced. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> retreated to remote zones and their leaders fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>. They fought by ambushes, raids, bombings and assassinations, while government death squads hunted prominent leftists.</p><p>Negotiations began in 1986, moderated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and led to an agreement on a political settlement signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1991. The final accord was signed before about 50,000 people in front of the National Palace of Culture, in the presence of Secretary-General Boutros Boutros-Ghali. The URNG leader Rolando Morán and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-arzu">Álvaro Arzú</a> shared the UNESCO Peace Prize, and the URNG later apologised to the victims of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>During the war the URNG drew on the rural poor and urban intellectuals; 82% of its demobilised combatants and half of its political activists were indigenous. It was closely tied to the Committee for Peasant Unity (CUC), founded on 15 April 1978. Registered as a party in December 1998, it entered the 1999 elections in the New Nation Alliance, whose candidate Álvaro Colom came third with about 12.4% of the vote. Its support later fell to a few seats in Congress.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Negotiations between the government and the <b>Guatemalan National Revolutionary Unity</b> (URNG), the guerrilla coalition formed in 1982, produced a series of accords culminating in the Agreement on a Firm and Lasting Peace of 29 December 1996, signed under President Álvaro Arzú.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Republic of Guatemala · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
